# PAM stack for null-lock, the session-lock screen locker (NULL.md §8.5).
#
# It authenticates the already-logged-in user to UNLOCK the screen. It opens no
# session and changes no password, so only `auth` (verify the secret) and
# `account` (the account is still valid) are stacked -- the two the client calls
# (pam_authenticate then pam_acct_mgmt, both of which must pass).
#
# We include `password-auth`, NOT `system-auth`. The two are Fedora's shared
# stacks and are identical for our purposes EXCEPT that `system-auth` also runs
# the interactive console-login modules -- notably `pam_fprintd.so` (fingerprint),
# ordered `sufficient` before `pam_unix`. On a machine with a fingerprint reader
# (e.g. a ThinkPad), that module engages during pam_authenticate and BLOCKS for
# ~30s waiting for a finger swipe -- which the lock client, a password-only UI,
# can never supply -- before falling through to the password check. The screen
# appears frozen and "won't unlock". `password-auth` is the password-only sibling
# stack (same pam_unix, same account rules, same faillock policy, no fingerprint),
# which is exactly right for a locker that only ever reads a typed password.
# (Fingerprint-at-unlock would need the client to drive the fprintd conversation;
# that is a possible post-1.0 feature, not a regression here.)
#
# Without this file the client's PAM handle would resolve to /etc/pam.d/other,
# which on Fedora denies everything -- every password would be refused and the
# machine could never be unlocked by its own locker. It must ship.
auth       include      password-auth
account    include      password-auth
